    "narrative": "Our lineup is anchored today by **Corbin Carroll** (83.65) and **Seiya Suzuki** (75.03), both of whom present as elite daily starts. **Corbin Carroll** benefits from a massive 19% boost due to the favorable SP_xERA of Canning, and his position in the #3 hole ensures maximum run-producing opportunities. Similarly, **Seiya Suzuki** is in a prime spot against Rogers; he leverages a 16% park factor advantage and an opportunistic matchup against a slower pitcher, which historically elevates his stolen base potential\u2014a critical factor given our league's scarcity of speed. \n\nWe are keeping a close watch on **Brice Turang** (64.44); while he remains a strong start, there is a 38% rain risk that could compromise his afternoon. His value is tied heavily to his ability to exploit the slow delivery of Pallante for stolen bases, a key component of his efficiency score. At the other end of the spectrum, **Jose Caballero** (41.61) and **Cole Young** (45.44) are teetering near our floor. **Jose Caballero** faces a steep challenge against a high-skill arm in Rasmussen, resulting in a 16% penalty to his score, while **Cole Young** suffers from a lack of lineup leverage in the #7 spot. Our most difficult bench decision involved **Edwin Arroyo** (36.27), who fell short of the 40-point threshold; despite being a switch hitter with a park advantage against Luzardo, his low spot in the batting order and the elite nature of the opposing pitcher rendered him an inefficient start today. \n\nWe are also monitoring the availability of our bench depth, specifically **James Wood**, **Mickey Moniak**, and **Trevor Story**, who are all currently unavailable due to off-days or IL status. As we grind through this 2026-07-09 slate, we remain prepared to rotate in assets like **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, or **Isaac Paredes** if late-breaking lineup changes force a pivot. For now, we trust the ATC projections for **Gabriel Moreno**, **Josh Naylor**, **Travis B